When `OrderService` creates `StripeGateway` internally with `new`, the two classes are permanently married. Every time the payment layer changes, the service that shouldn't care about it breaks.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final StripeGateway gateway = new StripeGateway();

    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        gateway.charge(order.getTotal());
    }
}

// Problem 1: Unit testing — StripeGateway hits the real network.
// Problem 2: Swap to PayPal — must modify OrderService source code.
// Problem 3: Staging env — no way to inject SandboxGateway without code change.
// Problem 4: StripeGateway changes its constructor — OrderService compile error.
```

All four problems share the same root: `OrderService` decided what it needed instead of declaring what it needs.

Replacing `new StripeGateway()` with a constructor parameter flips control. `OrderService` now declares what it needs (`PaymentGateway` — a contract), and Spring provides the matching implementation.

```java
public interface PaymentGateway {
    void charge(double amount);
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway;

    public OrderService(PaymentGateway gateway) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
    }

    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        gateway.charge(order.getTotal());
    }
}

// Unit test — no Spring context, no network:
var service = new OrderService(amount -> System.out.println("mock: " + amount));

// Production: @Component StripeGateway implements PaymentGateway — Spring wires it.
// Staging:    @Profile("staging") @Component SandboxGateway — Spring picks the right one.
```

`OrderService` now depends on a contract. Swap providers, write tests, configure environments — the service class never changes.

Most Spring teams use Lombok's `@RequiredArgsConstructor` to eliminate constructor boilerplate while keeping `final` fields.

**Lombok `@RequiredArgsConstructor`**
- Generates a constructor for every `final` field
- Combined with Spring 4.3+ single-constructor auto-detection, no `@Autowired` annotation needed
- The 'field injection is more concise' argument disappeared in 2015

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway;
    private final InventoryClient inventory;
}
```

**Kotlin primary constructors**
- Spring treats Kotlin primary constructors identically to Java constructor injection
- `class OrderService(private val gateway: PaymentGateway)` is constructor-injected with no annotations
- `val` keyword makes the field immutable by default

**Why this works the same across JVM languages**
- Constructor injection is a **design pattern**, not a Java-specific trick
- Spring 4.3+ auto-detects the sole constructor of a class for injection — no annotation required

`@Autowired` is not magic — it is a `BeanPostProcessor` (`AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor`) that runs reflection against each bean after instantiation and injects collaborators before `@PostConstruct`. This minimal reproduction shows the exact sequence.

```java
@Component
class OrderService {
    private final UserRepository userRepo;       // constructor injection (preferred)
    @Autowired private PaymentClient payment;    // field injection (discouraged)
    private EmailService email;

    public OrderService(UserRepository userRepo) { this.userRepo = userRepo; }

    @Autowired
    public void setEmail(EmailService email) { this.email = email; }  // setter injection
}

// The processor Spring actually runs — simplified reproduction:
class MiniAutowiredProcessor implements BeanPostProcessor {
    private final BeanFactory bf;
    MiniAutowiredProcessor(BeanFactory bf) { this.bf = bf; }

    @Override
    public Object postProcessBeforeInitialization(Object bean, String name) {
        for (Field f : bean.getClass().getDeclaredFields()) {
            if (f.isAnnotationPresent(Autowired.class)) {
                f.setAccessible(true);
                try { f.set(bean, bf.getBean(f.getType())); }    // inject by type
                catch (IllegalAccessException e) { throw new BeanCreationException(name, e); }
            }
        }
        return bean;   // return AFTER injection, BEFORE @PostConstruct
    }
}
```

- Spring picks the **greediest satisfiable constructor** for constructor injection — no annotation needed when there is only one.
- Resolution order: by **type** first, then by **qualifier** (`@Qualifier("name")`), then by **bean name** as a fallback.
- When multiple beans match, Spring throws `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` unless one is marked `@Primary`.
- Prefer constructor injection: fields can be `final`, circular dependencies fail fast at startup, and unit tests can call the constructor directly without `ReflectionTestUtils`.

The typical pattern is to mark one implementation as `@Primary` — the default that most injection points receive — and use `@Qualifier` only at injection points that need a specific alternative.

**Setup — primary plus qualified beans**

```java
public interface Notifier {
    void send(String message);
}

@Primary
@Component
public class EmailNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String message) { /* send email */ }
}

@Component("smsNotifier")
public class SmsNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String message) { /* send SMS */ }
}
```

**Default consumer — gets the @Primary**

```java
@Service
public class AlertService {
    public AlertService(Notifier notifier) { ... }
}
```

**Specialized consumer — overrides @Primary with @Qualifier**

```java
@Service
public class EmergencyService {
    public EmergencyService(@Qualifier("smsNotifier") Notifier notifier) { ... }
}
```

**The naming trap**
- `@Qualifier` value must match the bean name **exactly**
- Default bean name = class name with lowercase first letter
- `@Qualifier("SmsNotifier")` fails at startup with `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException`
- `@Component("customName")` overrides the default name

Beyond `@Primary` and `@Qualifier`, Spring has one more fallback: **field or parameter name matching**.

**The silent fallback**
- `private Notifier smsNotifier` matches a bean named `smsNotifier` even without `@Qualifier`
- Works but creates fragile coupling between variable names and bean registration
- Rename the field and injection silently breaks

**Custom qualifier annotations — the scalable approach**
- Build a meta-annotation that carries `@Qualifier`
- Compile-time safe — typos caught by the compiler
- Survives refactoring and IDE rename operations

```java
@Qualifier
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target({ElementType.FIELD, ElementType.PARAMETER, ElementType.TYPE})
public @interface Stripe {}

@Stripe @Component
public class StripeGateway implements PaymentGateway { ... }

@Service
public class CheckoutService {
    public CheckoutService(@Stripe PaymentGateway gateway) { ... }
}
```

**Why custom annotations win in production**
- String-based qualifiers fail only at runtime on a typo
- A `@Stripe` meta-annotation catches typos at compile time
- Common in production codebases with multiple database connections, messaging providers, or external API clients

The `${property.key}` syntax resolves against Spring's `Environment`, which aggregates properties from `application.properties`, environment variables, system properties, and command-line arguments. Spring walks these sources in priority order and returns the first match.

**Resolution timing**
- Happens at **bean creation time**, not at method call time
- The value is injected once and cached in the field
- If the property changes in the file after startup, the bean still holds the old value
- Live reload requires Spring Cloud Config's `@RefreshScope`

**Basic usage**

```java
@Service
public class PaymentClient {
    @Value("${payment.api.url}")
    private String apiUrl;

    @Value("${payment.timeout:5000}")
    private int timeoutMs;

    @Value("${payment.retries:3}")
    private int maxRetries;
}
```

**The colon-space trap**
- The colon separates key and default value
- **No space after the colon** — `${key: default}` injects `" default"` with a leading space
- Causes silent bugs in URLs and numeric parsing
- Always write `${key:default}` without spaces

While `${}` performs simple property lookup, `#{}` evaluates a **Spring Expression Language** expression. SpEL can call methods, access system properties, reference other beans, and perform arithmetic.

**Common SpEL patterns**

```java
@Value("#{systemProperties['java.version']}")
private String javaVersion;

@Value("#{T(Runtime).getRuntime().availableProcessors()}")
private int cpuCores;

@Value("#{'${allowed.origins}'.split(',')}")
private List<String> allowedOrigins;
```

**Combined ${} inside #{}**
- `#{'${key}'.split(',')}` — first resolves the property placeholder, then applies SpEL to the result
- Standard pattern for converting a comma-separated property into a `List`
- Note the nesting: `${}` is **inside** `#{}`, never the reverse

**Failure modes**
- SpEL in `@Value` is evaluated at startup
- If the expression fails — null property, class not found, missing method — `SpelEvaluationException` and the application will not start
- Keep expressions simple
- For complex logic, move it to a `@PostConstruct` method where you can handle errors gracefully

The moment you have three or more `@Value` fields sharing a prefix — `${mail.host}`, `${mail.port}`, `${mail.username}` — you should migrate to `@ConfigurationProperties`.

**What `@ConfigurationProperties` adds over `@Value`**
- Binds an entire prefix to a POJO
- Fields map to properties automatically
- Nested objects, lists, and maps work without SpEL
- Add `@Validated` and use **JSR-303 annotations** (`@NotBlank`, `@Min`, `@Max`) — validates at startup
- Missing required property fails with a clear message listing what is wrong

```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "mail")
@Validated
public class MailProperties {
    @NotBlank private String host;
    @Min(1) @Max(65535) private int port = 587;
    private Duration connectionTimeout = Duration.ofSeconds(30);
}
```

**Built-in conversions**
- Spring parses `Duration` values like `20s`, `5m`, or `PT30S` automatically
- `DataSize` parses `100MB`, `2GB`
- No conversion code needed

**IDE autocomplete from metadata**
- Spring Boot's annotation processor generates `spring-configuration-metadata.json`
- IDEs auto-complete your custom properties
- Your team stops guessing property names — the IDE tells them
- Justifies migration alone for any property group used by more than one developer
